\title{Text Encoding in Quantum Natural Language Processing: A Systematic Survey}

\author{\uppercase{Danilo Cavaliere}\authorrefmark{1}, \uppercase{Giuseppe Fenza}\authorrefmark{1}, \uppercase{Lukasz Gajewski}\authorrefmark{1}, and \uppercase{Vincenzo Loia}\authorrefmark{1}}

\address[1]{Department of Management \& Innovation Systems, Università degli Studi di Salerno, Italy}

\markboth{TEXT ENCODING IN QUANTUM NATURAL LANGUAGE PROCESSING}{TEXT ENCODING IN QUANTUM NATURAL LANGUAGE PROCESSING}

\corresp{Corresponding authors: Danilo Cavaliere and Lukasz Gajewski (e-mails: \{dcavaliere, lgajewski\}@unisa.it).}

\begin{abstract}
Quantum natural language processing (QNLP) applies superposition, entanglement, and interference to the representation and processing of linguistic information. Any QNLP pipeline, whatever methods it uses, must first map discrete or distributed text representations onto qubit registers or parameterized-circuit inputs. This encoding step governs model expressivity, qubit resource requirements, and trainability. Yet it is rarely studied as an independent variable, and no standard method brings these evaluation aspects together for analyzing and comparing encodings. This article aims to fill this gap by introducing a classification scheme for text encodings, covering both the encoding used and the experimental evidence reported for it, and employs it to carry out a systematic literature review of text encoding strategies in QNLP. The scope is limited to the encoding step: circuit design, ansatz expressibility, and error correction are covered only where they interact directly with encoding choices. The survey covers 125 studies published between 2021 and 2026, using a protocol based on state-of-the-art guidelines to define the search strategy, eligibility criteria, screening procedure, snowball sampling, and data extraction. The scheme is organized around a unifying taxonomy for analyzing and comparing encodings. Seven encoding families are identified and characterized: four major families, angle, amplitude, syntactic (DisCoCat), and structured feature maps (IQP/ZZ), and three emerging families: data re-uploading, hybrid classical--quantum embedding, and Hamiltonian/observable encoding. Their distribution is analyzed across NLP tasks, simulation and hardware environments, qubit budgets, and reporting practice. Three findings stand out: noiseless circuit simulation dominates the literature, real-hardware execution remains rare, and evaluation is too fragmented across author-chosen datasets, baselines, and metrics for results to be compared between studies. The encoding bottleneck, the dimensionality mismatch between linguistic representations and near-term qubit capacities, is stated explicitly as a basis for resource-efficient QNLP algorithm and hardware design.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keywords}
Encoding bottleneck, parameterized quantum circuits, quantum machine learning, quantum natural language processing, systematic literature review, text encoding.
\end{keywords}

\titlepgskip=-15pt

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
\PARstart{E}{very} quantum natural language processing (QNLP) system begins with a mandatory step: converting text into a quantum state. Before a single gate is applied or a single parameter is trained, a token, sentence, or document must be mapped onto qubits. This mapping, the encoding, determines how many qubits are consumed and how deep the resulting circuit must be. It determines whether gradients can be computed efficiently, and whether any genuinely quantum property, such as entanglement, is brought to bear on the linguistic content. It is the most consequential design decision in the pipeline.

Despite this centrality, encoding is rarely the subject of dedicated study: effort concentrates instead on other stages of the pipeline, such as ansatz design \cite{ruskanda_simple_2023,abiwardani_generalized_2025} and the choice of classical optimizer \cite{rajashekharaiah_sentence_2023}, or on carrying QNLP to a new task or domain \cite{abbaszadeh_hybrid_2026,kesiku_unlocking_2026}. Most QNLP papers dispatch it in a paragraph, introduce one strategy without justification, and move on to reporting accuracy numbers. After five years of accelerating publication activity, the result is a fragmented body of work: dozens of encoding families coexist, yet there is no common approach for analyzing and comparing them. Unified taxonomies, systematic comparisons, and a shared understanding of encoding trade-offs are all missing from the literature. Existing QNLP surveys and reviews do not isolate encoding as a variable \cite{chen_survey_2024,pallavi_quantum_2025}. The few explicitly comparative studies contrast model families or ans\"atze rather than encoding schemes \cite{bouakba_comparative_2025,castillo_comparative_2025}. This article fills that gap: it conducts an extensive systematic literature review of the topic and makes text encoding in QNLP the primary subject of study.

\subsection{Scope}
This survey reviews research that (1) applies a genuine quantum circuit (simulated noiseless, simulated noisy, or executed on real hardware) to at least one NLP task, and (2) identifies, at least by name or type, the encoding scheme used to map text or text-derived features onto qubits or circuit parameters. The review follows an SLR protocol defined before the search (Section~\ref{sec:method}) adapted from established guidelines for systematic literature reviews in computer science \cite{keele2007guidelines}.

Papers that use quantum-inspired or quantum-motivated classical algorithms without instantiating an actual parameterized quantum circuit are excluded. Also excluded are survey and review papers, hardware-focused benchmarking studies that treat NLP datasets as a generic payload, and papers whose methodological detail is insufficient to identify the encoding scheme, according to the inclusion and exclusion criteria stated in Section~\ref{sec:criteria}. The final corpus comprises 125 studies.

The scope is limited to the encoding step. Quantum circuit design, ansatz expressibility, and quantum error correction are surveyed only where they interact directly with encoding choices. Classical NLP methods are discussed only as baselines.

\subsection{Research Questions}
\label{subsec:research_questions}

The review is organized around five research questions, each answered from the data extracted across the corpus rather than from the claims of individual studies:

\begin{itemize}
\item[RQ1)] Which text encoding strategies are used in QNLP, and by what principle can they be organized into families? (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy})

\item[RQ2)] How does the use of each family vary across model families and ans\"atze, and can an encoding be compared independently of the architecture hosting it? (Section~\ref{sec:comparative_analysis})

\item[RQ3)] How has the mix of encodings and architectures changed over the years the corpus spans? (Section~\ref{sec:temporal_evolution})

\item[RQ4)] Does the choice of encoding track the NLP task, and is there a task family whose structure gives a principled reason to prefer one encoding over another? (Section~\ref{sec:task_fit})

\item[RQ5)] How rigorously are encoding claims evaluated, in terms of execution regime, hardware validation, and reproducibility? (Section~\ref{sec:experimental_rigor})
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Key Contributions}
This article makes four contributions to the study of text encoding in QNLP:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[1)] A novel taxonomy of text encoding strategies, organized by the point at which text enters the quantum computation and covering seven encoding families (four major and three emerging) with their specialized variants.
\item[2)] A systematic review of 125 studies that apply or propose quantum text encodings across NLP tasks, conducted under a protocol defined before the search and grounded in established methodological guidelines for systematic literature reviews: the Keele and Charters procedure for systematic reviews in computing \cite{keele2007guidelines}, the PRISMA 2020 statement for reporting the selection process \cite{page2021prisma}, and Wohlin's guidelines for snowball sampling \cite{wohlin2014guidelines}.
\item[3)] A quantitative cross-study characterization of the corpus using the classification scheme defined in Section~\ref{subsec:analysis_framework}, spanning encoding-by-model-family, temporal, task-fit, and experimental-rigor analyses.
\item[4)] A characterization of the encoding bottleneck in light of the survey results: the gap between the dimensionality of the text representations studies start from and the qubit budgets they actually use is quantified over the corpus, and each encoding family is read as a choice of which cost absorbs that gap, discarded information, added circuit depth, or a classical burden relocated upstream. This characterization, together with the gaps documented across the review, yields a corpus-derived set of five open challenges and an actionable agenda for future work.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Related Work}
\label{subsec:related_work}
Several prior works survey the QNLP literature, but none treats text encoding as the central variable. Varmantchaonala et al.\ \cite{varmantchaonala_quantum_2024} provide the most comprehensive QNLP survey to date, covering quantum algorithms, circuit architectures, and NLP task categories; encoding appears only as an implementation detail. Guarasci et al.\ \cite{guarasci_quantum_2022} offer an early and influential overview of QNLP challenges and opportunities, from theoretical quantum-advantage arguments to hardware experiments, without isolating encoding as a dimension of comparison. Widdows et al.\ \cite{widdows_quantum_2024} examine word embeddings and sequential quantum models with more attention to representation trade-offs than any other general survey, but stop short of a systematic comparison. Bouakba and Belhadef \cite{bouakba_comparative_2025} experimentally compare compositional DisCoCat models against quantum neural networks; their compositional-versus-flat-vector axis overlaps with the taxonomy proposed here, though their scope is limited to two model families. Huzzat et al.\ \cite{huzzat_primer_2026} is the most encoding-aware prior work: it addresses the mismatch between text-representation dimensionality and the qubit capacity of NISQ classifiers, and treats classical compression as a prerequisite for stable training. It remains, however, a conceptual primer for a single model class rather than a systematic review.

Beyond QNLP itself, the quantum engineering literature has an established tradition of comprehensive subfield surveys, covering, for instance, quantum circuit compilation for NISQ devices \cite{kusyk2021survey}, quantum computing for finance \cite{egger2020quantum}, and entanglement routing in quantum networks \cite{abane2025entanglement}, the last of which likewise organizes its field through an explicit taxonomy of approaches. None of these addresses text encoding in QNLP, which is the gap the present survey fills within that tradition.

This survey complements these works by treating encoding as the primary unit of analysis, yielding the first quantitative cross-study characterization of how text is encoded in QNLP and at what resource cost.

\subsection{Structure}
The rest of the article is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:background} provides the background on quantum computing, compositional distributional semantics, and the role of encoding in QNLP. Section~\ref{sec:method} describes the systematic review protocol (search strategy, eligibility criteria, screening, and snowball sampling) and the data extraction and classification scheme used throughout the analysis. Sections~\ref{sec:taxonomy}--\ref{sec:experimental_rigor} then answer RQ1--RQ5 in turn. Section~\ref{sec:trends_challenges} synthesizes the trends, states the encoding bottleneck explicitly, and enumerates the open challenges and the future-work agenda. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

\subsection{Quantum Computing Primitives}

A quantum bit (qubit) occupies a superposition of the basis states $|0\rangle$ and $|1\rangle$:
\begin{equation}
|\psi\rangle = \alpha|0\rangle + \beta|1\rangle, \quad |\alpha|^2 + |\beta|^2 = 1,
\end{equation}
where $\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{C}$ are probability amplitudes. A register of $n$ qubits occupies a $2^n$-dimensional Hilbert space $\mathcal{H} = \mathbb{C}^{2^n}$; its state is a unit vector in that space. This exponential dimension is the property every amplitude-style encoding exploits. Entanglement introduces correlations with no classical counterpart, and measurement collapses the state to a classical bitstring, destroying superposition. Whether superposition, entanglement, or interference confers a practical benefit on NLP tasks remains the central open question of the field, and Section~\ref{sec:experimental_rigor} examines how far the surveyed evidence can speak to it.

\subsection{Parameterized Quantum Circuits}

The dominant computational model in near-term quantum machine learning is the parameterized quantum circuit (PQC), also called a variational quantum circuit \cite{cerezo2021variational}. A PQC applies a sequence of unitary gates to an initial state $|\psi_0\rangle$:
\begin{equation}
U(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = U_L(\boldsymbol{\theta}_L) \cdots U_1(\boldsymbol{\theta}_1),
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{\theta} \in \mathbb{R}^p$ are trainable parameters. Model outputs are expectation values $\langle O \rangle = \langle \psi_0 | U^\dagger(\boldsymbol{\theta})\, O\, U(\boldsymbol{\theta}) | \psi_0 \rangle$, and gradients follow analytically from the parameter-shift rule \cite{mitarai2018quantum, schuld2019evaluating}. PQCs underlie six of the seven encoding families surveyed here; the exception is grammatical encoding, which derives circuit structure from sentence syntax rather than from a fixed ansatz.

PQCs operate under the constraints of noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) hardware \cite{preskill2018quantum}: shallow circuits, limited qubit counts, and significant gate error rates. These constraints bear directly on the encoding step. Every qubit or gate layer spent loading a text representation is a resource unavailable to the learning task.

\subsection{Text Encoding}

Text encoding is the procedure that maps a text input $x$ (a token, sentence, or document) to a quantum state $|\phi(x)\rangle \in \mathcal{H}$ or to a parameter vector $\boldsymbol{\theta}(x)$ that initializes a PQC. It is the mandatory interface between classical language data and the quantum circuit. The input $x$ may be a raw token sequence, a classical embedding vector from a pre-trained model, or a parse tree carrying grammatical structure. Which representation is used, and how it is loaded onto qubits, defines the encoding family.

\subsection{Compositional Distributional Semantics and DisCoCat}

One of the two main traditions in QNLP originates in categorical linguistics. Coecke, Sadrzadeh, and Clark \cite{coecke2010mathematical} showed that the compact closed categorical structure of pregroup grammars also governs quantum processes in Hilbert space. In a pregroup grammar, each word carries a grammatical type, and a sentence is parsed by reducing compound types through tensor contractions. These reductions obey the same diagrammatic calculus as quantum processes, so a parse tree translates directly into a quantum circuit: words become parameterized quantum states, and grammatical reductions become cups in a string diagram.

This is the DisCoCat (Distributional Compositional Categorical) model, operationalized as an end-to-end pipeline by \cite{lorenz_qnlp_2023} and executed on near-term hardware in \cite{meichanetzidis_quantum_2021, meichanetzidis_grammar-aware_2023} via the \textsc{lambeq} toolkit. The circuit structure is not fixed in advance: the parse tree determines it, so syntactically distinct sentences produce structurally distinct circuits. This property sets the grammatical encoding family apart from all other families, which treat the sentence as a flat feature vector.

\subsection{Quantum Machine Learning for Text}

The second tradition approaches NLP as a classification problem. It applies quantum feature maps \cite{schuld2019quantum} and variational circuits \cite{cerezo2021variational} to text-derived feature vectors: classical embeddings (word2vec, GloVe, or transformer outputs) are compressed to the available qubit count, loaded into a PQC via rotation gates or amplitude encoding, and trained end-to-end on labeled examples. Because this approach assumes nothing about grammatical structure, it applies to arbitrary text without a parser and scales more easily to realistic dataset sizes. Its logical endpoint is the hybrid quantum--classical architecture, in which a large classical language model handles high-dimensional representation and a small quantum circuit acts as a downstream head; such architectures have grown in prevalence since 2023.

The two traditions differ in how they encode text and in what they claim the quantum component contributes. Compositional models argue for structural alignment between grammar and quantum computation; QML models argue for expressivity or kernel separability.

\section{Review Methodology}
\label{sec:method}

\Figure[t!](topskip=0pt, botskip=0pt, midskip=0pt)[scale=1]{figures/review_workflow.pdf}{Overview of the review workflow. Labels on the left give the subsection in which each step is described; Fig.~\ref{fig:prisma} gives the record-level detail of the search, screening, and snowball stages.\label{fig:workflow}}

\Figure[t!](topskip=0pt, botskip=0pt, midskip=0pt)[width=\textwidth]{PRISMA_Chart.jpg}{PRISMA flow diagram. Final corpus: $n = 125$ (95 from database search, 30 from snowball sampling).\label{fig:prisma}}

This systematic literature review followed the guidelines for systematic literature reviews in software engineering established by Keele and Charters \cite{keele2007guidelines}. The protocol was defined before the search was executed. It fixed the search strategy, eligibility criteria, screening procedure, snowball sampling, and data extraction process. Fig.~\ref{fig:workflow} gives an overview of the resulting workflow and of where each step is described below.

\subsection{Search Strategy}
\label{subsec:search}
Two complementary search queries were executed on two electronic databases, Scopus and IEEE Xplore, to cover both the quantum machine learning and the compositional QNLP literature. The first query targeted the intersection of quantum computing or machine learning with NLP and encoding-related terms. The second targeted QNLP-specific terminology: the DisCoCat and DisCoCirc frameworks, established pipelines that implement them such as the \textsc{lambeq} toolkit, and categorical linguistic concepts. The searches were restricted to English-language journal articles and conference papers published between 2021 and 2026 (ends included), excluding editorials, book chapters, and erratum notices. They returned 269 results from Scopus and 217 from IEEE Xplore, a combined total of 486 records. Removing 110 duplicates left 376 unique records.

\subsection{Eligibility Criteria}
\label{sec:criteria}

A study was selected if and only if it met all six inclusion criteria below.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{IC1: NLP task grounding.} The study applies or evaluates the proposed method on at least one natural language processing task, such as classification, similarity, sequence labeling, question answering, or generation.

    \item \textit{IC2: Genuine quantum computation.} The study implements and evaluates the method in at least a noiseless quantum circuit simulation (exact statevector simulation or equivalent) or on real quantum hardware; merely quantum-inspired classical algorithms, such as tensor-network models or classical approximations of quantum kernels, do not satisfy this criterion.

    \item \textit{IC3: Encoding scheme identified.} The study names or identifies at least the type of encoding used to map text or text-derived features onto qubits, quantum states, or circuit parameters; a named or typed mention is sufficient, and circuit diagrams or full mathematical derivations are not required.

    \item \textit{IC4: Peer-reviewed or preprint venue.} The study appeared in a peer-reviewed journal, conference, or workshop, or as a preprint with sufficient methodological detail to assess IC1--IC3.

    \item \textit{IC5: Language and access.} The study is available in full text, in English, and accessible for review.

    \item \textit{IC6: Time window.} The study was published between 2021 and 2026 inclusive.
\end{itemize}

Even if a study satisfied all six inclusion criteria, it was excluded if any of the following applied:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{EC1: No NLP application.} The study applies quantum machine learning exclusively to non-text domains and offers no transferable text-encoding discussion.

    \item \textit{EC2: Classical-only encoding.} The study uses encoding purely in the classical sense (tokenization, positional encoding, one-hot) with no quantum circuit involved.

    \item \textit{EC3: Insufficient detail.} The study is an abstract, extended abstract, or short paper that names an encoding but provides no formal description or reproducible detail.

    \item \textit{EC4: Duplicate or superseded.} The study is a conference version superseded by an extended journal version; only the more complete version was retained.

    \item \textit{EC5: Pure hardware focus.} The study addresses hardware benchmarking alone and uses an NLP dataset only as a generic payload, with no discussion of linguistic encoding properties.

    \item \textit{EC6: Quantum-inspired, classical surrogate, or unverifiable simulation.} The study uses quantum-derived mathematical formalism without instantiating an actual parameterized quantum circuit, reports only classical surrogates of quantum behavior, or claims quantum simulation without evidence of framework or circuit use.

    \item \textit{EC7: Survey or review paper.} The study is primarily a survey or review of existing work rather than an original research contribution; such papers were retained as reference material and are discussed in Section~\ref{subsec:related_work}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Screening and Selection}
\label{subsec:screening}

Screening proceeded in two stages. In the first stage, all 376 records were assessed on title and abstract alone for broad topical relevance to quantum computing applied to text. No IC/EC criteria were applied at this stage; its only purpose was to remove records clearly outside the scope of the review. This eliminated 234 records and left 142 reports sought for retrieval, of which 6 could not be retrieved, leaving 136 reports assessed for eligibility.

In the second stage, the full text of each of the 136 reports was assessed against the complete set of inclusion and exclusion criteria of Section~\ref{sec:criteria}. This yielded 95 papers for the corpus. The remaining 41 retrieved reports were excluded or retained as surveys, mainly under IC2 (no genuine quantum circuit; $n = 15$), IC3 (encoding scheme not identified; $n = 6$), EC6 (quantum-inspired, classical surrogate, or unverifiable simulation; $n = 3$), and EC7 (survey or review; $n = 11$); six records were excluded under other criteria.

\subsection{Snowball Sampling}
\label{subsec:snowball}
To reduce the risk of missing relevant work not indexed by the two databases, two rounds of backward and forward snowball sampling \cite{wohlin2014guidelines} were applied to the surveys excluded under EC7. Surveys were chosen as the seed set, rather than the included studies, for two reasons. First, Wohlin's guidelines ask for a start set that is small, diverse, and well connected to the target population; in a field as young as QNLP, the reference list of a survey is by construction a curated, near-exhaustive sample of the literature in the field up to its search date, so each survey seed contributes far more unique candidates than an individual study, whose citations concentrate on the same few foundational works. Second, forward snowballing from surveys exploits a stable citation practice: new studies routinely cite the reviews of the field, so the papers citing a survey are enriched for precisely the recent work that database indexing lags behind. The cost of this choice is that a study neither cited by nor citing any survey of the field remains invisible to the procedure; this residual risk is discussed in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}. Reference lists and citing papers yielded 1222 unique candidate records. Title-and-abstract screening for broad topical relevance reduced this set to 242 reports sought for retrieval, of which 37 could not be retrieved, leaving 205 reports assessed for eligibility. Full-text assessment against the complete IC/EC protocol then added 30 papers to the corpus. The remaining 175 retrieved reports were excluded or retained as surveys. The main grounds were IC1 (no NLP task; $n = 74$), IC2 (no genuine quantum circuit; $n = 52$), IC6 (outside the time window; $n = 20$), IC3 (encoding scheme not identified; $n = 4$), and EC7 (survey or review; $n = 22$); three records were excluded under other criteria.

\subsection{Final Corpus}
\label{subsec:corpus}
The final corpus comprises 125 papers, 95 from the database search and 30 from snowball sampling; Fig.~\ref{fig:prisma} summarizes the selection process as a PRISMA 2020 flow diagram \cite{page2021prisma}.

\subsection{Data Extraction and Classification}
\label{subsec:analysis_framework}

Each study in the corpus was classified along a fixed set of dimensions, so that every classification, cross-tabulation, and trend reported in Sections~\ref{sec:taxonomy}--\ref{sec:trends_challenges} follows directly from the extraction sheet rather than from an ad hoc reading of the corpus. The two primary dimensions are the \emph{encoding family}, defined by the point at which text or text-derived features enter the quantum computation (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}), and the \emph{model family}, defined by the architecture that consumes the encoded state. Secondary dimensions capture the resources and evidence attached to that choice: qubit count and whether qubit scaling was tested, execution regime, presence of a classical baseline, and whether scalability was discussed. Each dimension is operationalized as one or more columns of the structured extraction sheet compiled during data extraction.

Assignment follows explicit decision rules. A study is classified by its \emph{dominant} encoding interface: the one that carries the text representation into the circuit whose output is evaluated. Auxiliary encodings used only in ablations or baselines do not change the class; a study whose main pipeline is amplitude-encoded but that reports an angle-encoded ablation is classified as amplitude. Where a pipeline stacks a classical encoder in front of a quantum layer, the study is classified as \texttt{hybrid\_embed} only when the classical stage is co-trained or purpose-built as part of the contribution; a frozen pretrained embedding feeding a standard rotation layer remains \texttt{angle}. The \texttt{hybrid\_nn} model family covers any architecture in which a classical deep network (transformer, recurrent, or convolutional, not limited to LLM-based extractors) performs the main representational work and the quantum circuit is a submodule. A bare variational classifier over precomputed features is \texttt{vqc}, even when those features come from a deep model. Compositional (DisCoCat-style) studies are identified by circuit topology derived from a grammatical parse, regardless of toolkit. These disambiguation rules were fixed during protocol definition and applied uniformly during extraction.

Extraction and classification were performed by one author and reviewed by a second; disagreements were resolved by discussion against the decision rules above. All figures below are reported over the full corpus of 125 studies.

The corpus is small enough that many of the cross-tabulations below have cells in the low single digits, and every count is a complete enumeration of the corpus rather than an estimate from a sample of it. All distributions are therefore reported descriptively, as counts and shares of a known population. Where the article asserts that two dimensions are coupled rather than simply describing one, the claim rests on the size of the imbalance in the cross-tabulation together with a stated mechanism, and both are given. Percentages computed on denominators below ten are reported with their denominators and are not read as evidence of a difference.

\section{Taxonomy of Text Encoding Strategies}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

This section answers RQ1. The encoding problem in QNLP is not a single operation. Depending on the study, text can enter the quantum computation as rotation parameters, as amplitudes of a prepared state, as a grammar-derived circuit, as a structured feature map, or through a more specialized interface such as repeated data injection, a hybrid neural representation, or an observable. These mechanisms are often discussed under the same term, ``encoding,'' although they make different assumptions about where linguistic information is represented and which quantum resource is being used.

Table~\ref{tab:encoding_taxonomy} organizes these mechanisms into the proposed taxonomy, applying the classification rules of Section~\ref{subsec:analysis_framework}: every study discussed below is assigned to exactly one encoding family by the dominant-interface rule, based on the point at which text or text-derived features enter the quantum computation. The optimizer, measurement rule, downstream classifier, and ansatz are treated as design choices that can be combined with any encoding family.

The taxonomy comprises seven encoding families. Four are major: angle encoding ($n=51$), amplitude encoding ($n=28$), syntactic/DisCoCat encoding ($n=29$), and structured feature-map encoding ($n=9$). Three are emerging families that together account for the remaining eight studies: data re-uploading ($n=4$), hybrid classical--quantum embedding ($n=3$), and Hamiltonian/observable encoding ($n=1$). The main tradeoff across them is between width, depth, state-preparation cost, and the amount of linguistic structure preserved by the quantum circuit \cite{preskill2018quantum,cerezo2021variational,ruskanda_evaluating_2025}.

